{"qid":"Q199442","names":["Kingdom of Aragon"],"title":"Kingdom of Aragon","endonym":{"native":"Reino d'Aragón","lang":"an"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Aragon originated as a small county carved from the Carolingian March of the Pyrenees, becoming a kingdom under Ramiro I (r. 1035–1063), son of Sancho III of Navarre. Alfonso I 'the Battler' (r. 1104–1134) dramatically expanded it southward, capturing Zaragoza in 1118 and pushing the frontier to the Ebro; he also briefly united Aragon with Navarre. His successor Ramiro II 'the Monk' arranged the marriage of his daughter Petronilla to Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona, creating the Crown of Aragon (1137/1150), which superseded the independent kingdom; the data window closes at 1169, shortly after this dynastic union took hold.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The kingdom's territory is well documented from Aragonese, Navarrese, and Castilian chronicles and charters; frontier zones with the Taifa kingdoms were fluid during reconquest campaigns.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Aragon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Aragon"},{"label":"Britannica: Aragon","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Aragon"},{"label":"Britannica: Alfonso I — king of Aragon","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Alfonso-I-king-of-Aragon"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Aragon","rulers":[{"qid":"Q379792","name":"Ramiro I of Aragon","start":1035,"end":1063,"image":"Remiro I d'Aragón.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Remiro%20I%20d'Arag%C3%B3n.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"lic